For reference:
BAC = Base armor class, i.e. physical quality of the 
armor, separate from enchantment.
 
     I'd like to make a couple of contributions to the 
idea of destructive rust:
 
1) Weapons should perhaps be included in this scheme, 
using the weapon damage as the equivilant of the BAC of 
armor.  Thus, a dagger with a damage figure of 4 could get 
rusted four times before being destroyed, and unless it 
was negatively enchanted, it would do 1-3 damage after the 
first rust, 1-2 after the second, and thus never do 0 
damage due to rust unless it was *so* badly rusted that it 
was ineffective as a weapon (-4 due to rust).  Hit ability 
should most likely not be affected by rust.
     I see two problems with this:
a) Small vs. large monster damages
b) Things such as the 2-H sword which are 3d6 rather than 
1d(something).
     Perhaps we should simply leave weapons out of the 
whole scheme for the sake of simplicity.
 
2) Anyone who has found a rust trap would be able to 
summarily dispose of cursed armor by taking off all other 
armor and putting away his/her weapon and repeatedly 
stepping into the trap.  This strikes me as too easy.  One 
can do the same thing with nymphs, but you have to hide 
all your stuff from them, which is quite a bit more risky 
and/or difficult.
     Thus, cursed armor should rust until it has a BAC of 
0 and then stop there.  "Your shield looks quite rusted.", 
for instance.
 
3) Since your magical adjustment would remain separate 
from the base armor value, rusting would be permanent, 
i.e. If I get my plate mail rusted down from a BAC of 7 to 
a BAC of 1, I can't enchant it back up so it's worth as 
much as a normal plate mail without risking destruction 
(or, rather, I can, if I'm lucky enough to get it to go 
from 3 to 6, but that's as far as it will go); in 
addition, this fully functioning plate mail will be 
destroyed with one rust attack.  Now, insofar as that may 
be realistic, it's okay, but it's not especially playable, 
since part of the game balance relies on balancing out 
rust attacks with enchant scrolls (usually in reverse
order, since most armor starts out unenchanted and can be 
replaced if its enchantment becomes subzero).
     So, to counter this, a potion of (what, tarnish 
remover?) might be added in to counter rust effects, 
probably in units of 1-2 rusts (50% for either) for an 
uncursed potion, the whole extent of damage for a blessed 
potion, and of course a cursed potion is overly acidic and 
actually damages the armor or weapon (BAC, of course).  A 
blessed potion applied to undamaged *and unenchanted* 
(either positive or negative, since cursed or negatively 
enchanted armor or weapons would be likely to reject 
improvements and positively enchanted equipment is already 
assumed to be of fine physical form; besides, it makes the 
game a bit harder) weapons or armor could even increase 
the BAC by one, although never more than one more than the 
standard BAC of that particular piece of armor.
 
     I think play balance and realism would both be 
preserved by these three additions (assuming destructive 
rust were added in as well).
     Also, to return to 3), the potion might act as a cure
disease potion if quaffed, or it might simply *BE* holy water
(i.e. added to the effects of holy water).  If it is in fact
a separate potion, it doesn't necessarily need to have anyt
special effect when quaffed, I suppose.


